    body: "\u2022 Lung toxicity (pneumonitis, interstitial lung disease) is a recognised adverse effect of the drug classes on the cards that carry it: BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase inhibitors (imatinib mesylate, dasatinib); Bruton tyrosine kinase (BTK) inhibitors (ibrutinib); Phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (PI3K) inhibitors (idelalisib, duvelisib); FLT3 kinase inhibitors for acute myeloid leukemia (midostaurin); Immunomodulatory drugs (thalidomide analogues) (lenalidomide); Proteasome inhibitors (bortezomib, carfilzomib); EGFR tyrosine kinase inhibitor (6 drugs); ALK or ROS1 kinase inhibitor (alectinib hydrochloride, brigatinib, lorlatinib, crizotinib, ceritinib); ALK or ROS1 kinase inhibitor / TRK inhibitor (repotrectinib); MET kinase inhibitor (capmatinib, tepotinib hydrochloride); RET kinase inhibitor (selpercatinib, pralsetinib); KRAS G12C inhibitor (sotorasib, adagrasib); MEK inhibitor (trametinib dimethyl sulfoxide, binimetinib); VEGFR-targeting kinase inhibitors (sorafenib tosylate, pazopanib hydrochloride, vandetanib); mTOR inhibitors (everolimus, temsirolimus); CDK4/6 inhibitors (palbociclib, ribociclib, abemaciclib); PI3K and AKT pathway inhibitors (alpelisib); HER2 tyrosine kinase inhibitors (lapatinib); PARP inhibitors (olaparib, niraparib); Estrogen receptor antagonists (tamoxifen citrate); Androgen synthesis inhibitor (CYP17) (abiraterone acetate); Androgen receptor inhibitors (apalutamide, bicalutamide); GnRH agonists (leuprolide acetate); Anti-CD20 antibodies (rituximab); Anti-HER2 antibodies (trastuzumab, pertuzumab); Anti-EGFR antibodies (cetuximab, panitumumab); Bispecific EGFR-MET antibody (amivantamab-vmjw); HER2-directed antibody-drug conjugate (ado-trastuzumab emtansine, fam-trastuzumab deruxtecan-nxki); Trop-2-directed antibody-drug conjugate (datopotamab deruxtecan-dlnk); Vedotin antibody-drug conjugate (MMAE payload) (enfortumab vedotin-ejfv, brentuximab vedotin, tisotumab vedotin-tftv, telisotuzumab vedotin-tllv); Calicheamicin antibody-drug conjugate (gemtuzumab ozogamicin); Folate receptor alpha-directed antibody-drug conjugate (DM4 payload) (mirvetuximab soravtansine-gynx).\n\u2022 The drug's own label instruction (withhold, permanently discontinue, named rescue agent, monitoring threshold), where the label gives one, is on the drug's card under this effect.\n\n**Related consults (differential, overlap and supportive care; the drug's label instruction is on its card):**\n\u2022 [Shortness of Breath Hub](#/tree/dyspnea-hub/node/dysp-start): breathlessness\n\u2022 [Cyanosis / Hypoxaemia Hub](#/tree/acute-cyanosis-hypoxemia-hub/node/cyanosis-start): low oxygen\n\u2022 [Oxygen Delivery Systems](#/tree/oxygen-delivery/node/o2-start): oxygen support\n\u2022 [ARDS](#/tree/ards/node/ards-start): if ARDS\n\u2022 [Pneumonia](#/tree/pneumonia/node/pna-start): differential: infection\n\u2022 [PE Treatment](#/tree/pe-treatment/node/pe-start): differential: pulmonary embolism\n\u2022 Two pages elsewhere are specific to other drugs, not these: [Checkpoint inhibitor pneumonitis (Oncological Emergencies)](#/tree/oncological-emergencies/node/onc-ici-pneumonitis) and [Bleomycin and other chemotherapy lung toxicity](#/node/odse-tox-chemo-pulmonary) (cytotoxic chemotherapy).\n\n**Not covered:** The app has no consult for drug-induced interstitial lung disease; the card carries the label's steroid or withhold instruction where the label gives one.\n\n**Clinical note (myMedKitt editor, not from the cited sources):** disposition is decided with the treating oncologist.",
